# Alatyr specification — licensing

Copyright (c) 2026 Nick Nizovtsev and the Alatyr specification contributors.

This repository holds a **specification**, not an implementation, so it is licensed as
two things at once. Which license applies depends on *what you take*, not on which file
you took it from — the two kinds of material are interleaved inside the same chapters.

## 1. The specification text — CC BY 4.0

Every prose, table, grammar production, diagram, and normative rule in this repository —
that is, the whole of `spec/`, `design/`, `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and this file, minus
the code fragments described in §2 — is licensed under the

**Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license (CC BY 4.0)**,
full text in [`LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0`](LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0),
summary: <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>.

You may copy, quote, republish, translate, and build upon the specification, including
commercially, provided you give appropriate credit ("the Alatyr language specification",
with a link to this repository), indicate whether you made changes, and do not suggest
that the Alatyr project endorses your derivative.

**Attribution and conformance are separate things.** CC BY grants you the right to
*reuse the text*. It grants no right to call an implementation "conforming"; conformance
is defined by the specification itself (Overview §6) and is a factual claim about
behavior, not a license grant.

## 2. Code fragments — Apache-2.0

Every code fragment in this repository — Alatyr source examples, `package.al` manifest
snippets, assembly and WAT listings, emitted-code samples, command lines, and any file
that may later be added under a directory of examples — is *additionally* licensed under
the

**Apache License, Version 2.0**,
full text in [`LICENSE-APACHE-2.0`](LICENSE-APACHE-2.0),
summary: <https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0>.

This exists so that an implementation can lift a fragment straight out of a chapter into
its own test suite, conformance corpus, or standard library without a CC BY attribution
notice riding along in the source, and so that the fragments carry Apache-2.0's explicit
patent grant.

At your option you may instead use a code fragment under CC BY 4.0; Apache-2.0 is offered
as the more convenient of the two for code, not as a restriction on it.

## 3. Contributions

Unless you state otherwise in the contribution itself, any contribution you intentionally
submit for inclusion in this repository is licensed under the same dual terms above, with
no additional conditions — Apache-2.0 §5 applies to the code-fragment part.

## 4. Names

The names "Alatyr" and "Алатырь" and the `.al` extension are not licensed by either
license above; they identify the project. You may use them to refer
truthfully to this specification and to your implementation of it (for example, "an
Alatyr compiler", "conforms to the Alatyr specification 1.0.0"), but not in a way
that suggests your project is the official one.
